### CBBC Protocol v0.2

### Conceptual Byzantine Behavioral Consensus

Project: Xenograph Analytics Architecture: Epistemic Modernization Integrity Protocol classification: Hybrid Byzantine Fault Tolerant Behavioral Consensus Version: 0.2 Status: Proposed engineering specification

### 1\. Protocol mandate

CBBC v0.2 establishes a distributed protocol for converting articulated substrate values into enforceable technological behavior.

The architecture combines:

* Equal-weight Byzantine consensus voting.

* Specialized validator responsibilities.

* Dual authorization for behavioral policy changes.

* Evidence-based execution authorization.

* Cryptographic message authentication.

* Quorum-certified state transitions.

* Automatic suspension when integrity failures are detected.

* Persistent audit and recovery mechanisms.

### 1.1 Architectural decisions

| Decision                      | Selected architecture             |
| ----------------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| Validator architecture        | Hybrid                            |
| Voting authority              | Equal voting weight               |
| Verification responsibilities | Specialized                       |
| Behavioral policy authority   | Dual authorization                |
| Integrity failure response    | Suspend                           |
| Consensus foundation          | PBFT-style BFT                    |
| Execution authority           | Quorum + behavioral authorization |
| Record preservation           | Cryptographic persistence         |

Foundational rule:

A validator may independently contribute evidence, verification, and computational execution, but no individual validator can unilaterally authorize a system-wide state transition.

The protocol separates three authorities:

* Governance authority: Approves the rules.

* Consensus authority: Establishes agreement.

* Execution authority: Executes only an authorized operation.

### 2\. Hybrid validator architecture

The hybrid model combines equal voting rights with specialized verification responsibilities.

Every active consensus validator has one vote, regardless of its computational resources, financial contribution, or specialized role.

Specialization determines what a validator must verify, not how much voting power it possesses.

### 2.1 Validator classifications

| ID  | Role                 | Required responsibility                      |
| --- | -------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |
| GOV | Governance Validator | Policy authorization and version control     |
| EVD | Evidence Validator   | Provenance and evidence integrity            |
| BHV | Behavioral Validator | Policy compliance                            |
| EXE | Execution Validator  | Resource availability and execution validity |
| INT | Integrity Validator  | Hashes, signatures, and state consistency    |
| ORC | Oracle Validator     | External information and source attestations |
| AUD | Audit Validator      | Decision records and audit preservation      |

These are logical capabilities. A validator can perform multiple capabilities, but critical checks must have independently administered verification paths.

### 2.2 Voting structure

### Equal-weight hybrid consensus

