A specialized validator cannot override the BFT quorum. Conversely, a BFT quorum cannot override a mandatory failed behavioral or integrity check.

### 2.3 Validator independence

The implementation should distinguish:

* Validator identity.

* Validator operator.

* Infrastructure provider.

* Signing key.

* Verification capabilities.

* Voting eligibility.

* Administrative control domain.

This prevents an organization from appearing to operate multiple independent validators when all of them actually depend on the same administrator or infrastructure.

### 3\. Behavioral policy authority — dual authorization

Behavioral policies define the permitted operational boundaries of the network.

A policy change must receive two distinct forms of authorization:

AUTHORITY A — GOVERNANCE APPROVAL

Authorized policy approval and cryptographic signature

AUTHORITY B — DISTRIBUTED VALIDATOR CONSENSUS

BFT quorum approval of the exact policy version and hash

POLICY ACTIVATION

Approved policy becomes effective at its designated epoch

Neither authority is sufficient independently.

### 3.1 Policy lifecycle

* Draft policy.

* Validate policy syntax and permitted operations.

* Obtain governance approval.

* Generate canonical policy hash.

* Distribute the proposed policy to validators.

* Verify the governance signature.

* Run the BFT consensus process.

* Commit the policy activation record.

* Activate the policy at the designated epoch.

* Preserve the previous version for audit and recovery.

### 3.2 Policy immutability during consensus

Every consensus proposal must reference:

* Policy identifier.

* Policy version.

* Policy hash.

* Activation epoch.

Validators must reject proposals referencing unknown, superseded, or unauthorized policy versions.

No policy may be modified in place after activation. Changes create a new version.

### 4\. Protocol message formats

All consensus messages must use a canonical serialization format, authenticated signatures, and domain-separated cryptographic hashes.

The following JSON structures define the proposed logical message schema. A production implementation should use a canonical encoding such as deterministic CBOR or JSON Canonicalization Scheme (JCS), rather than signing arbitrary JSON text.

### 4.1 Common message envelope

Every message uses a shared envelope.

```json

{
  "protocol": "CBBC",
  "version": "0.2",
  "network_id": "XENOGRAPH-01",
  "message_type": "PREPARE",
  "message_id": "unique-message-id",
  "sender": "VALIDATOR-001",
  "epoch": 1,
  "view": 0,
  "sequence": 1001,
  "timestamp": "2026-11-13T00:00:00Z",
  "payload_hash": "sha256:...",
  "signature": "digital-signature"
}
```

Required fields:

| Field        | Purpose                              |
| ------------ | ------------------------------------ |
| protocol     | Identifies CBBC                      |
| version      | Protocol compatibility               |
| network_id   | Prevents cross-network message reuse |
| message_type | Identifies the operation             |
| message_id   | Message identification               |
| sender       | Authenticated validator identity     |
| epoch        | Consensus configuration period       |
| view         | Current leader/view number           |
| sequence     | Ordered operation number             |
| timestamp    | Diagnostic and audit metadata        |
| payload_hash | Cryptographic payload commitment     |
| signature    | Authenticated message origin         |

The signature must cover the canonical message contents, including the payload hash and domain separation information. A timestamp alone must never determine message validity.

